<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Chengpu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">632 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Chengpu was fought in 632 BCE in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> between the State of Jin, under Duke Wen, with its allies Qi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, and the forces of Chu and its allies. Jin's victory, won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a> and attacks on the weaker Chu wings, halted Chu's advance into the north and made Duke Wen hegemon of the Zhou states. The battle is described in detail in the Zuo zhuan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-chengpu.jpg" alt="Battle of Chengpu" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Chengpu.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-huan-of-qi">Duke Huan of Qi</a> in 643 BCE, the State of Chu steadily extended its influence northward, absorbing half a dozen smaller states as satellites. In 636 BCE Chong'er, a prince of Jin, returned after nineteen years in exile and came to power as Duke Wen of Jin with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-mu-of-qin">Duke Mu of Qin</a>. In the winter of 633 BCE King Cheng of Chu attacked the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, Jin's ally, and the following spring Duke Wen marched south and occupied Wey and Cao, both satellites of Chu.</p><p>As Duke Wen had promised King Cheng during his exile in Chu, the Jin army withdrew three days' march before camping on the plain of Chengpu, which also joined it with its Qi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> reinforcements. Only the Chu centre under the prime minister Ziyu was made up wholly of Chu troops; its right wing came from Chen and Cai, its left from Shen and Xi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The armies met on the fourth day of the fourth month of 632 BCE. Xu Chen, commander of the Jin left, dressed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> horses in tiger skins to panic the Chu horses and scattered the weak Chu right wing. His men then held the Chu centre in place. On the Jin right, Hu Mao's wing skirmished and faked a retreat, carrying off the commander-in-chief's two great banners, while Luan Zhi's chariots dragged branches to raise a cloud of dust that hid them as they wheeled and reformed.</p><p>Believing the Jin right beaten, the Chu left pursued and was caught in the flank by Duke Wen's bodyguards and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> contingent, then struck by Hu Mao's returning wing and destroyed. With both wings enveloped, Ziyu ordered a general retreat, which became a rout when his men found that the Qi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> contingents had captured the Chu camp and train in the rear. Duke Wen did not direct the fighting himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Chengpu was the first major battle in the long conflict between the states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> valley and those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, and one of the biggest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a>. On his return north Duke Wen was recognised by the King of Zhou as first among the feudal lords, and a conference of states he led at Jiantu in 631 BCE swore a covenant of alliance. The victory did not, however, restrict the power of Chu in the long term.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Chengpu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jin's victory at the <b>Battle of Chengpu</b> in 632 BCE halted Chu's advance to the north, but King Zhuang (r. 613–591 BCE) defeated Jin at the Battle of Bi in 597 BCE, while Jin won again at Yanling in 575 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke Xian expanded Jin by conquering neighbours such as Western Guo and the State of Yu, and Duke Wen, the long-exiled Chong'er, became hegemon after defeating the State of Chu at <b>Chengpu</b> in 632 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-jin">State of Jin</a></li></ul></section>
